WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.850
JEN PERSON: This week, we're
talking about VMware, database

00:00:02.850 --> 00:00:04.970
choices, and modernization.

00:00:04.970 --> 00:00:07.830
I'm Jen Person and welcome to
"This Week in Cloud," where

00:00:07.830 --> 00:00:10.890
we serve you the
lowest latency news.

00:00:10.890 --> 00:00:12.750
Customers have been
asking us to provide

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:14.220
broad support for VMware.

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:17.050
And now with Google
Cloud VMware solution,

00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:20.220
you'll be able to run VMware
[? vSphere ?] based workloads

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:21.360
in GCP.

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:23.250
This means you have
more choices for how

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:26.210
to run VMware workloads
in a hybrid deployment,

00:00:26.210 --> 00:00:28.710
from modern containerized
applications with Anthos

00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:32.369
to VM-based applications
with VMware in GCP.

00:00:32.369 --> 00:00:35.520
And should those workloads
happen include databases,

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:38.220
we've made a few upgrades
on that front too.

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:41.120
Enjoy Cloud SQL from
Microsoft SQL Server,

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:44.640
federated queries from BigQuery
to Cloud SQL, and the ability

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:47.610
to run Elastic Cloud
on GCP in Japan.

00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:50.700
To learn more about how each
of these upgrades can help you,

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:52.680
check out the full post.

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:56.220
One thing you're sure to need,
no matter what, security.

00:00:56.220 --> 00:00:57.720
Read up on some
recent improvements

00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:00.510
we made, from our advanced
protection program

00:01:00.510 --> 00:01:02.970
to ML threat detection
to new locations

00:01:02.970 --> 00:01:05.220
around the globe where our
tightened security keys can

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:07.620
be used on the cloud blog.

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:10.470
But first you've got to get
your workloads modernized

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:12.060
and into the cloud.

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:14.040
And that's why we've
also added new tools

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:15.810
to make the journey
even smoother,

00:01:15.810 --> 00:01:18.450
like support for
migrating from Azure

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:21.540
and tools to manage networking
for your microservices,

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:24.840
like traffic detector, now
generally available, and Layer

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:28.200
7 internal load
balancer, now in beta.

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:32.447
See why each one makes such a
big difference on the GCP blog.

00:01:32.447 --> 00:01:34.530
If you want to read more
about these announcements

00:01:34.530 --> 00:01:37.300
or see a full list,
check out the link.

00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:39.040
Have any thoughts or ideas?

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:40.960
Leave us a comment or tweet us.

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:43.760
We'll see you in the cloud.

